      "writtenOn": "2023-01-16T05:51:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is different from what we do today. The very first method that is called is setHandles. That is what initializes the HAL daemon and passes in the fds. initOffload is only called later.\n\nSee OffloadController#start, which does:\n\n    public boolean start() {\n        ...\n        if (!mConfigInitialized) {\n            mConfigInitialized \u003d mHwInterface.initOffloadConfig();\n            if (!mConfigInitialized) {\n                mLog.i(\"tethering offload config not supported\");\n                stop();\n                return false;\n            }\n        }\n\n        mControlHalVersion \u003d mHwInterface.initOffloadControl(\n                // ...\n                new OffloadHardwareInterface.ControlCallback() {\n                    @Override\n                    public void onStarted() {\n\nIn OffloadHardwareInterface, initOffloadConfig gets the service, opens the netlink fds, and calls setHandles.\n\nBecause we expect the same code to drive both the old and the new HAL, I think it\u0027s better to ensure that the ordering of the calls is the same between the two HALs. That\u0027s why I suggested that setHandles be renamed to initialize or some such - it\u0027s the first method that needs to be called, and that should be clear from the method name. Or we could get rid of that call altogether, and just pass the FDs as arguments to initOffload.\n\nThis test will be impacted because we have separate TetheroffloadAidlPreInitTest and TetheroffloadAidlGeneralTest classes. Not sure what to do about that. Perhaps we could leave all the tests as is except for this one, and add a test simply have a test that ensures that initOffload throws IllegalStateException if initialize (previously called setHandles) has not already been called? But this test may be impossible for a real HAL implementation to pass, because once setHandles has been called, there is no way to clear the fds and uninitialize the HAL. Maybe we can just delete this test?",
